// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  CONTRACT B — a second foreign-language term, driven to a DIFFERENT normal form.
//
//  Run me with the Rholang interpreter:
//    target/debug/rholang demos/flt-assay-desk/contract-b.rho
//
//  THE TERM — the mirror combinator discards its FIRST argument
//  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//        C = lam a. lam b. b      the MIRROR   combinator (= λ.λ.0)  "keep the SECOND argument"
//        I = lam x. x             the IDENTITY combinator (= λ.0)
//
//  This file is the redex
//        (C I) C
//  the exact mirror image of Contract A's `(K I) K`, and it takes the same two β steps —
//        (C I) C   ⟶β   (λb. b) C   ⟶β   C
//  — but lands somewhere else, because C keeps its SECOND argument where K keeps its first.
//  Same shape, same cost, different answer: that is what makes the desk's filter in
//  `desk-accepts-constant.rho` a real decision rather than a formality.
//
//  EXPECT
//  ──────
//        normal form on @"OUT"  =  ⟦λ.λ.0⟧                the mirror combinator C
//        ^fired ledger          =  ["Beta", "Beta"]       2 in-Rho rewrite firings
//        ^drive-err / ^drive-fuel empty                   ⟹ it terminated by QUIESCENCE
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

lambda:Term`((lam a. lam b. b, lam x. x), lam a. lam b. b)`
